<%# SoliDB landing page — "receipts, not adjectives". Every capture on this page
    is copied from a release post and links to it, and every claim about
    behaviour (the clustering facts included) was checked against the source.
    When you add one, add its source, or leave it out.
    Styling lives in app/assets/css/application.css (LANDING section). No user
    content here, so nothing needs escaping. %>

<header class="nav">
  <div class="wrap nav-inner">
    <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="SoliDB home">SoliDB <%- partial("shared/version_pill", {}) %></a>

    <nav class="nav-links" aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="#perf">Performance</a>
      <a href="#cluster">Clustering</a>
      <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
      <a href="/docs">Docs</a>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a>
      <a href="/docs/changelog">Changelog</a>
    </nav>

    <div class="nav-right">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm" href="https://github.com/solisoft/solidb">GitHub</a>
      <%- partial("shared/theme_toggle", {}) %>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<main>

<!-- ============================ HERO ============================ -->
<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap hero-grid">
    <div>
      <h1>Seven data models in one server.</h1>

      <%# One row per model: its name, and the SDBQL that reads it. A model
          belongs here once it has a docs page and a query form of its own;
          the count in the h1 and in share_title (layouts/application) is
          the number of rows. %>
      <ul class="models" aria-label="The seven data models">
        <li><a href="/docs/documents"><b>Documents</b><code>FOR … FILTER … RETURN</code></a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/graphs"><b>Graphs</b><code>1..3 OUTBOUND</code></a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/vector-search"><b>Vectors</b><code>VECTOR_SIMILARITY</code></a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/sdbql-functions-search"><b>Full-text search</b><code>FULLTEXT · BM25</code></a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/timeseries"><b>Time series</b><code>TIME_BUCKET</code></a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/columnar"><b>Columnar tables</b><code>COLLECT … AGGREGATE</code></a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/sdbql-functions-geo"><b>Geo</b><code>GEO_DISTANCE</code></a></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="hero-lede">
        All seven, and blobs beside them, live in one solistore store behind one
        query language, SDBQL. The captures on this page come from real servers.
        Each one names the release it shipped in and links to the post that
        shows how it was taken.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/getting-started">Read the getting-started guide</a>
        <a class="btn-link" href="/docs/changelog">What changed in the latest release</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-meta">O'Saasy License · Rust, on solistore · HTTP, WebSocket and a MessagePack driver</p>
    </div>

    <%# Slideshow of captured sessions. Every slide is copied from a release
        post that ran it on a real server; the caption says which, and says
        "abridged" where lines were cut. Add a slide only with its source.
        Script at the bottom of this file; without JS the first slide shows. %>
    <section class="capture slideshow" aria-roledescription="carousel" aria-label="Captured from real servers" data-slideshow>
      <div class="slide-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Features">
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="st-tx" aria-controls="sl-tx" aria-selected="true">Transactions</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="st-col" aria-controls="sl-col" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Columnar</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="st-ts" aria-controls="sl-ts" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Time series</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="st-roles" aria-controls="sl-roles" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Roles</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="st-sec" aria-controls="sl-sec" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Security</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="st-repl" aria-controls="sl-repl" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Replication</button>
      </div>

      <div class="slides">
        <figure class="slide is-active" id="sl-tx" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="st-tx" aria-roledescription="slide">
          <div class="capture-title">A transaction that should fail, failing.</div>
          <pre><span class="dim"># users has a unique index on email</span>
POST …/transaction/$TX/query
  <span class="tok-kw">INSERT</span> {_key:<span class="tok-str">"u1"</span>, email:<span class="tok-str">"a@x.io"</span>} <span class="tok-kw">INTO</span> users   <span class="dim">→ staged</span>
POST …/transaction/$TX/query
  <span class="tok-kw">INSERT</span> {_key:<span class="tok-str">"u2"</span>, email:<span class="tok-str">"a@x.io"</span>} <span class="tok-kw">INTO</span> users   <span class="dim">→ staged</span>
POST …/transaction/$TX/commit
<span class="hit">{"code":400,"type":"InvalidDocument",
 "error":"Unique constraint violated:
   'uniq_email:046140782e696f00' is claimed
   by both 'u1' and 'u2' in this transaction"}</span>

<span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> u <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> users <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> u._key   <span class="dim">→ "result":[]</span></pre>
          <figcaption>
            Release 2.1.0. Captured on a throwaway server. Before 2.1.0 both inserts
            committed; now the commit fails as a whole and writes nothing.
            Source: <a href="/blog/solidb-2-1-transactions-conflicts-roles#unique">/blog/solidb-2-1-transactions-conflicts-roles</a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="slide" id="sl-col" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="st-col" aria-roledescription="slide">
          <div class="capture-title">Columnar rows joined to documents, in one query.</div>
          <pre><span class="dim"># metrics is a columnar collection, hosts a document one</span>
<span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> m <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> metrics
  <span class="tok-kw">FILTER</span> m.cpu &gt; <span class="tok-num">50</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> h <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> hosts
    <span class="tok-kw">FILTER</span> h._key == m.host
    <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> {region: h.region, cpu: m.cpu}

<span class="out">→ [{"cpu": 58.0, "region": "eu-west"},
   {"cpu": 72.5, "region": "eu-west"},
   {"cpu": 90.0, "region": "us-east"}]</span></pre>
          <figcaption>
            Release 0.33.0. Every result in the post was produced on a throwaway server.
            Source: <a href="/blog/columnar-collections-in-sdbql#first-class">/blog/columnar-collections-in-sdbql</a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="slide" id="sl-ts" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="st-ts" aria-roledescription="slide">
          <div class="capture-title">Days with no orders still get a row.</div>
          <pre><span class="tok-kw">LET</span> per_day = <span class="tok-fn">COUNT_BY</span>((<span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> o <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> orders <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> o),
                       o -&gt; <span class="tok-fn">DATE_TRUNC</span>(o.at, <span class="tok-str">"day"</span>))
<span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> d <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> <span class="tok-fn">DATE_SERIES</span>(<span class="tok-str">"2024-03-01"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"2024-03-04"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"day"</span>)
  <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> {day: <span class="tok-fn">LEFT</span>(d, <span class="tok-num">10</span>), orders: per_day[d] || <span class="tok-num">0</span>}

<span class="out">→ [{"day": "2024-03-01", "orders": 2},
   {"day": "2024-03-02", "orders": 0},
   {"day": "2024-03-03", "orders": 1},
   {"day": "2024-03-04", "orders": 0}]</span></pre>
          <figcaption>
            Release 1.3.0. Produced on a 1.3.0 server.
            Source: <a href="/blog/sdbql-1-3-new-functions#reports">/blog/sdbql-1-3-new-functions</a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="slide" id="sl-roles" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="st-roles" aria-roledescription="slide">
          <div class="capture-title">A role that stops at one database.</div>
          <pre>POST /_api/auth/users/bob/roles
  {"role":"editor","database":"v"}
<span class="dim">→ 201</span> {"username":"bob","role":"editor","database":"v", …}

bob reads   v                <span class="dim">→</span> 200
bob writes  v                <span class="dim">→</span> 200
bob reads   other            <span class="dim">→</span> <span class="hit">403</span>
bob writes  other            <span class="dim">→</span> <span class="hit">403</span>
bob creates a database       <span class="dim">→</span> <span class="hit">403</span></pre>
          <figcaption>
            Release 2.1.0. Captured on a throwaway server.
            Source: <a href="/blog/solidb-2-1-transactions-conflicts-roles#roles">/blog/solidb-2-1-transactions-conflicts-roles</a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="slide" id="sl-sec" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="st-sec" aria-roledescription="slide">
          <div class="capture-title">Code the server runs cannot be written by name.</div>
          <pre><span class="dim"># ana holds the editor role</span>
curl -X POST …/_api/database/blog/document/_scripts \
  -H "authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -d '{"name":"pwn","path":"pwn","code":"return 1"}'

<span class="hit">→ HTTP 403
{"code": 403,
 "error": "Access denied: '_scripts' is managed by the
   server and is not writable through this API; use
   the dedicated admin endpoints",
 "type": "Forbidden"}</span></pre>
          <figcaption>
            Release 1.1.0. Captured on a throwaway server; request abridged.
            Source: <a href="/blog/secure-by-default#demo">/blog/secure-by-default</a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="slide" id="sl-repl" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="st-repl" aria-roledescription="slide">
          <div class="capture-title">A second node joins and serves the first one's data.</div>
          <pre><span class="dim"># n2 log</span>
Sent join request to 127.0.0.1:6923
Successfully joined cluster. Received 2 peers.
Starting full sync: 1 databases, 7 documents
Full sync complete, final sequence: 5

<span class="dim"># query on n2</span>
<span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> n <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> notes <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> {key: n._key, from: n.from}
<span class="out">→ [{"from": "n1", "key": "hello"}]</span></pre>
          <figcaption>
            Release 0.34.0. Real nodes started on one host; log abridged.
            Source: <a href="/blog/clustering-across-machines#walkthrough">/blog/clustering-across-machines</a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="slide-controls">
        <button type="button" class="slide-btn" data-prev aria-label="Previous example"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7"></path></svg></button>
        <span class="slide-count" aria-live="polite"><span data-index>1</span> / 6</span>
        <button type="button" class="slide-btn" data-next aria-label="Next example"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"></path></svg></button>
        <button type="button" class="slide-btn slide-play" data-toggle aria-label="Pause the slideshow" aria-pressed="false"><svg class="ico-pause" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="7" y="5" width="3.5" height="14" rx="1"></rect><rect x="13.5" y="5" width="3.5" height="14" rx="1"></rect></svg><svg class="ico-play" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 5.5v13l11-6.5z"></path></svg></button>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========================== PERFORMANCE ========================== -->
<section class="sec" id="perf">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>Measured against the databases built for each job.</h2>
      <p>
        Five workloads, each against the databases people choose for it, on one machine (an AMD Ryzen 9
        9950X), each database alone in a container of 4 cores and 16&nbsp;GB. Every chart starts at 0 and
        has its own scale, and each tab links to the post that gives every figure, the harness, and where
        SoliDB is behind.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="perf-durable">
      <h3>What an acknowledged write survives</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>A transaction commit</dt><dd>Acknowledged after its fsync, at every isolation level but
          <code>read_uncommitted</code>. A process kill loses nothing, and neither does a power loss.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>A write outside a transaction</dt><dd>By default, acknowledged once handed to the operating
          system: a process kill loses nothing, a power loss can lose the last 100&nbsp;ms. With <code>SOLIDB_DURABILITY=full</code>
          it waits for its fsync, which concurrent writes share.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>What was tested</dt><dd>120 <code>kill -9</code> under 32 clients on 2.9.4: 15.8 million
          acknowledged transfers, none lost, none half applied. A power loss has not been tested.
          <a class="text-link" href="/blog/durability-and-transactions">Durability and transactions</a>.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="perf-tabs slide-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Benchmarks" data-perf-tabs hidden>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="pt-documents" aria-controls="perf-documents" aria-selected="true">Documents</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="pt-columnar" aria-controls="perf-columnar" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Columnar</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="pt-graph" aria-controls="perf-graph" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Graph</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="pt-timeseries" aria-controls="perf-timeseries" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Time series</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="pt-transactions" aria-controls="perf-transactions" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Transactions</button>
    </div>

    <div class="perf-panel" id="perf-documents" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pt-documents">
      <h3 class="perf-title">Documents, against PostgreSQL, MongoDB, ArangoDB and SurrealDB</h3>
      <p class="perf-lead">
        The same million orders and the same queries. SoliDB 2.9.0, on its solistore engine, led 21 of the 23
        series. PostgreSQL won the other two: a join with one client, and inserts fsynced before they are
        acknowledged.
        <a class="text-link" href="/blog/solidb-against-postgresql-mongodb-arangodb-surrealdb">How it was
        measured, every figure and the harness</a>.
      </p>

    <%# perf:start. Generated from the results by benchmarks/compare/post_figures.py (home:perf). %>
<div class="shapes">
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>One order by its key</b><span>requests/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="One order by its key, requests/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">733,576</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.515" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">378,048</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.411" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">301,150</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.323" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">236,629</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.100" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">73,585</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>A customer&rsquo;s orders</b><span>requests/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="A customer&rsquo;s orders, requests/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">220,404</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.805" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">177,403</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.418" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">92,129</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.168" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">36,984</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.090" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">19,941</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Join: 20 orders, customers</b><span>requests/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Join: 20 orders, customers, requests/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">68,784</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.643" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">44,221</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.233" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">16,010</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.263" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">18,123</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.139" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">9,589</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Full-scan COLLECT</b><span>ms per query, lower is better</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Full-scan COLLECT, ms per query, lower is better">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.039" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">37.3 ms</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.073" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">69.9 ms</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.160" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">153.8 ms</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">959.3 ms</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.227" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">218.0 ms</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Insert one order</b><span>requests/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Insert one order, requests/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">158,777</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.892" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">141,703</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.593" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">94,169</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.408" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">64,753</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.071" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">11,292</span></span></li>
</ol>
<p class="perf-cap">Insert runs vary more than reads: the fastest of a database&rsquo;s three runs is ahead of its slowest by 26% for SoliDB, 15% for PostgreSQL, 15% for MongoDB and 14% for ArangoDB. The bars are the medians.</p>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Insert one order, fsynced</b><span>requests/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Insert one order, fsynced, requests/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.907" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">81,298</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">89,587</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.676" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">60,579</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.205" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">18,371</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.115" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">10,272</span></span></li>
</ol>
<p class="perf-cap">Insert runs vary more than reads: the fastest of a database&rsquo;s three runs is ahead of its slowest by 33% for PostgreSQL, 43% for MongoDB and 223% for ArangoDB. The bars are the medians.</p>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Update one field</b><span>requests/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Update one field, requests/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">183,742</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.598" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">109,955</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.628" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">115,389</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.242" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">44,489</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.060" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">11,012</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Load 1M orders</b><span>documents/s, 8 connections</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Load 1M orders, documents/s, 8 connections">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">471,251</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.642" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">302,399</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>MongoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.483" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">227,521</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ArangoDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.499" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">235,190</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>SurrealDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.116" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">54,486</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
    <%# perf:end %>

      <p class="perf-note">
        <b>Durability.</b> No database fsyncs each write in these charts, except in <i>Insert one order,
        fsynced</i>, where each one does before it acknowledges (SoliDB with
        <code>SOLIDB_DURABILITY=full</code>, PostgreSQL with <code>synchronous_commit=on</code>).
        Medians of three 20-second runs, November 9, 2026. PostgreSQL is the faster of its jsonb and
        typed-column setups in each chart, except the fsynced inserts, measured on jsonb only. The full
        scan is quickest on SoliDB because it splits it across the 8 threads of its container; counted in CPU
        time per query, MongoDB and PostgreSQL spend less. SoliDB runs on solistore, which is not yet its
        default engine.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="perf-panel" id="perf-columnar" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pt-columnar">
      <h3 class="perf-title">Columnar, against ClickHouse and DuckDB</h3>
      <p class="perf-lead">
        Ten million events of six columns and four analytical questions. SoliDB has the higher figure in the
        eight series, at one client and at four: over ClickHouse by 24% to sixteen times, over DuckDB by 38%
        to eleven times. DuckDB loads the rows in 1.9 seconds, inside the process that queries it; SoliDB
        takes 7.1 and ClickHouse 7.2, as JSON over HTTP.
        <a class="text-link" href="/blog/solidb-against-clickhouse-on-a-columnar-table">The post, with what a
        table of four questions does not say about ClickHouse</a>.
      </p>

<div class="shapes">
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>One country: count and sum</b><span>requests/s, 4 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="One country: count and sum, requests/s, 4 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">2,616</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ClickHouse</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.500" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">1,307</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>DuckDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.088" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">230</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Every row, grouped by country</b><span>requests/s, 4 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Every row, grouped by country, requests/s, 4 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">231</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ClickHouse</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.218" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">50.3</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>DuckDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.727" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">168</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>A time window, grouped by device</b><span>requests/s, 4 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="A time window, grouped by device, requests/s, 4 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">3,196</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ClickHouse</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.060" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">193</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>DuckDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.437" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">1,397</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Top 10 of 100,000 users by total</b><span>requests/s, 4 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Top 10 of 100,000 users by total, requests/s, 4 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">49.3</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>ClickHouse</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.428" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">21.1</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>DuckDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.475" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">23.4</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>

      <p class="perf-note">
        <b>Durability.</b> These are reads. The rows were loaded with SoliDB at its default: a write is
        acknowledged once handed to the operating system and fsynced within 100&nbsp;ms. Medians of three
        20-second runs, November 15, 2026. SoliDB is a build of the branch that became 2.11.0. ClickHouse 26.10.4 reads a <code>MergeTree</code> table ordered by
        <code>(country, ts)</code>, the order that suits the first question.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="perf-panel" id="perf-graph" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pt-graph">
      <h3 class="perf-title">Graph, against Memgraph, Neo4j and ArangoDB</h3>
      <p class="perf-lead">
        One graph of a million people and ten million edges, six questions, each database in its own query
        language. SoliDB is ahead on the twelve series; the narrowest lead is three hops at one client, 9%
        over Memgraph. Loading the graph with its indexes takes SoliDB 24 seconds with a 2&nbsp;GB write
        buffer and 46 with its default, behind ArangoDB&rsquo;s 27.
        <a class="text-link" href="/blog/solidb-against-neo4j-memgraph-arangodb-on-a-graph">The post, with
        the same questions under writes</a>.
      </p>

<div class="shapes">
