</div>

      <p class="perf-note">
        <b>Durability.</b> These are reads. The graph was loaded with SoliDB at its default: a write is
        acknowledged once handed to the operating system and fsynced within 100&nbsp;ms. Medians of three
        20-second runs, November 14, 2026, on the build that became 2.10.4. SoliDB answers part of a
        traversal from what earlier queries left in memory. Two of Memgraph&rsquo;s figures come from
        queries someone who knows it better may write differently: its shortest path (<code>*BFS</code>)
        searches from one end only, and the purchase pattern is enumerated path by path.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="perf-panel" id="perf-timeseries" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pt-timeseries">
      <h3 class="perf-title">Time series, against QuestDB and TimescaleDB</h3>
      <p class="perf-lead">
        8,640,000 points in 1,000 series and five questions. SoliDB has the highest figure in eight series of
        ten; QuestDB has the latest point and the insert at eight clients, and loads the points in 1.3
        seconds where SoliDB takes 7.9 and TimescaleDB 7.4.
        <a class="text-link" href="/blog/solidb-against-questdb-timescaledb-on-time-series">The post, with
        where QuestDB is ahead</a>.
      </p>

<div class="shapes">
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>One series over one hour</b><span>requests/s, 8 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="One series over one hour, requests/s, 8 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">211,715</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>QuestDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.296" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">62,763</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>TimescaleDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.130" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">27,420</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Five-minute averages over six hours</b><span>requests/s, 8 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Five-minute averages over six hours, requests/s, 8 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">57,773</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>QuestDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.244" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">14,105</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>TimescaleDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.117" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">6,779</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>The latest point of a series</b><span>requests/s, 8 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="The latest point of a series, requests/s, 8 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.854" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">289,577</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>QuestDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">338,912</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>TimescaleDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.470" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">159,400</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Every series over ten minutes</b><span>requests/s, 8 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Every series over ten minutes, requests/s, 8 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">187,967</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>QuestDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.689" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">129,426</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>TimescaleDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.007" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">1,232</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Insert one point</b><span>requests/s, 8 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Insert one point, requests/s, 8 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.817" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">204,467</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>QuestDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">250,359</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>TimescaleDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.331" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">82,846</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>

      <p class="perf-note">
        <b>Durability.</b> The inserted points are not fsynced one by one: SoliDB runs at its default (a
        write is acknowledged once handed to the operating system and fsynced within 100&nbsp;ms),
        TimescaleDB with <code>synchronous_commit=off</code>, QuestDB with its write-ahead log at its
        defaults. Medians of three 20-second runs, November 15, 2026. SoliDB is a build of the branch that
        became 2.11.0. QuestDB keeps its worker threads busy with one client as
        with eight: the charts compare answers per second, not answers per core.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="perf-panel" id="perf-transactions" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pt-transactions">
      <h3 class="perf-title">Transactions, against PostgreSQL</h3>
      <p class="perf-lead">
        A bank: each transfer debits an account, credits another and writes a ledger row, in one transaction,
        <b>fsynced before it is acknowledged</b> on both databases. Sent as one request, PostgreSQL commits
        1.22 times as many transfers at one client and 1.14 times as many at 32. SoliDB is ahead over five
        round trips at 32 clients, and commits 3.2 times as many among ten hot accounts.
        <a class="text-link" href="/blog/durability-and-transactions">The post: what a commit promises,
        the kills and the isolation levels</a>.
      </p>

<div class="shapes">
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>One request per transfer</b><span>transfers/s, 1 client</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="One request per transfer, transfers/s, 1 client">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.818" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">17,728</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">21,674</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>One request per transfer</b><span>transfers/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="One request per transfer, transfers/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.877" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">99,963</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">113,920</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Five requests per transfer</b><span>transfers/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Five requests per transfer, transfers/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">71,889</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.937" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">67,383</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="shape"><div class="shape-head"><b>Ten hot accounts, one request</b><span>transfers/s, 32 clients</span></div>
<ol class="perf-rows" aria-label="Ten hot accounts, one request, transfers/s, 32 clients">
<li class="perf-row is-solidb"><span>SoliDB</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 1.000" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">33,474</span></span></li>
<li class="perf-row"><span>PostgreSQL</span><span class="perf-track"><span class="perf-bar" style="--w: 0.311" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="perf-val">10,406</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>

      <p class="perf-note">
        <b>Durability.</b> Every commit counted here was fsynced first: SoliDB 2.9.4 on solistore with
        <code>SOLIDB_DURABILITY=full</code>, PostgreSQL 18.6 with <code>synchronous_commit=on</code>.
        The same bank was killed with <code>SIGKILL</code> 120 times while 32 clients transferred: of 15.8
        million acknowledged transfers, none was lost and none was half applied. Medians of three 20-second
        runs, November 9 to 11, 2026. One request is <code>run_transaction</code> on SoliDB and a PL/pgSQL
        function on PostgreSQL; five requests are SoliDB&rsquo;s driver and PostgreSQL at
        <code>READ COMMITTED</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========================== CLUSTERING ========================== -->
<section class="sec" id="cluster">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>A cluster is a peer and a keyfile.</h2>
      <p>
        A new node names one existing member with <code class="mono">--peer</code>. It
        learns the other members from that member's answer, then pulls the data that
        was written before it joined. HTTP and replication share one port. The
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/cluster">cluster</a> and
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/sharding">sharding</a> docs have the rest.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cluster">
      <div class="cluster-main">
      <pre class="well"><span class="dim"># once, then copy the file to every machine</span>
$ openssl rand -hex 32 &gt; solidb.key

<span class="dim"># the seed, on 10.0.0.1: no --peer</span>
$ solidb --host 10.0.0.1 --keyfile solidb.key

<span class="dim"># every other node names the seed</span>
$ solidb --host 10.0.0.2 --keyfile solidb.key \
    --peer 10.0.0.1:6745
$ solidb --host 10.0.0.3 --keyfile solidb.key \
    --peer 10.0.0.1:6745

<span class="dim"># ask more than one node who it sees</span>
$ curl -u admin:… 10.0.0.3:6745/_api/cluster/status</pre>

      <figure class="cluster-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 480 284" role="img" aria-labelledby="cl-t">
          <title id="cl-t">The three nodes the commands start: two joiners dial the seed, the seed answers each with the member list and a full sync, and the joiners learn about each other from that answer</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="cl-arr-join" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="cl-head-join" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker>
            <marker id="cl-arr-sync" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="cl-head-sync" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker>
          </defs>

          <path class="cl-join" d="M52,208 L196,82" marker-end="url(#cl-arr-join)"/>
          <path class="cl-sync" d="M222,82 L78,208" marker-end="url(#cl-arr-sync)"/>
          <path class="cl-join" d="M428,208 L284,82" marker-end="url(#cl-arr-join)"/>
          <path class="cl-sync" d="M258,82 L402,208" marker-end="url(#cl-arr-sync)"/>
          <path class="cl-learn" d="M156,248 L324,248"/>

          <text class="cl-label" x="104" y="132" text-anchor="end">--peer</text>
          <text class="cl-label cl-label-sync" x="168" y="164">full sync</text>
          <text class="cl-label" x="376" y="132">--peer</text>
          <text class="cl-label cl-label-sync" x="312" y="164" text-anchor="end">full sync</text>
          <text class="cl-note" x="240" y="240" text-anchor="middle">learned from db1</text>

          <rect class="cl-node cl-seed" x="170" y="16" width="140" height="64" rx="4"/>
          <rect class="cl-node" x="16" y="210" width="140" height="64" rx="4"/>
          <rect class="cl-node" x="324" y="210" width="140" height="64" rx="4"/>
          <text class="cl-name" x="186" y="42">db1 · seed</text>
          <text class="cl-addr" x="186" y="64">10.0.0.1:6745</text>
          <text class="cl-name" x="32" y="236">db2</text>
          <text class="cl-addr" x="32" y="258">10.0.0.2:6745</text>
          <text class="cl-name" x="340" y="236">db3</text>
          <text class="cl-addr" x="340" y="258">10.0.0.3:6745</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The commands above, drawn. Each joiner dials only the seed; the seed answers
          with the member list and a full copy of the data written before the join.
          Every message on these lines is signed with the same <code>solidb.key</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="cluster-facts">
        <div class="cluster-fact">
          <h3>No keyfile, no cluster.</h3>
          <p>A node started with <code>--peer</code> and no <code>--keyfile</code> refuses to start. Every cluster message is signed with HMAC-SHA256 over a timestamp and a nonce, and is rejected if it is more than five minutes off the receiver's clock. The key signs traffic but does not encrypt it, so keep the port on a private network.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cluster-fact">
          <h3>It will not advertise an address no peer can reach.</h3>
          <p>With a peer on another host, a node that would give out <code>0.0.0.0</code>, <code>::</code> or loopback stops at startup. If you bind every interface, name the routable one with <code>--advertise</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cluster-fact">
          <h3>Sharding is chosen per collection.</h3>
          <p>Set <code>numShards</code> and <code>replicationFactor</code> when you create a collection, and each document goes to the shard its key hashes to. A collection created without them is replicated whole to every node.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cluster-fact">
          <h3>A dead node's shards get new replicas.</h3>
          <p>Nodes send heartbeats every 5 s. A node silent for 10 s is suspected, and after 15 s it is marked dead. Every 5 s each node re-creates missing replicas on healthy members. When a node joins or comes back, <code>POST /_api/cluster/rebalance</code> spreads the shards out again.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cluster-fact">
          <h3>Sharded collections have limits.</h3>
          <p>A filter on an indexed field scans every shard. <code>If-Match</code> is refused. A write that would change a document's shard-key value is refused, because documents do not move between shards.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cluster-fact">
          <h3>A single node pays nothing for it.</h3>
          <p>Since 2.0.3, a node with no peers does not write the replication log until a peer first reads from it. That log write cost about 40% of a primary-key update.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ======================== ONE QUERY SHAPE ======================== -->
<section class="sec" id="query">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>The same query shape for every model.</h2>
      <p>
        <code class="mono">FOR … RETURN</code> walks a graph, ranks vectors and buckets a
        time series. The query text is shown without invented output: run it against
        your own data with the <a class="text-link" href="/docs/sdbql">SDBQL reference</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="shapes">
      <div class="shape">
        <div class="shape-head"><b>Graph</b><span>1 to 2 hops out</span></div>
        <pre class="well"><span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> v, e <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> <span class="tok-num">1</span>..<span class="tok-num">2</span> <span class="tok-kw">OUTBOUND</span> <span class="tok-str">'people/alice'</span> follows
  <span class="tok-kw">FILTER</span> e.since &gt; <span class="tok-num">2020</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> { person: v.name, since: e.since }</pre>
      </div>
      <div class="shape">
        <div class="shape-head"><b>Vector</b><span>bind the query embedding as @vec</span></div>
        <pre class="well"><span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> doc <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> articles
  <span class="tok-kw">LET</span> sim = <span class="tok-fn">VECTOR_SIMILARITY</span>(doc.embedding, <span class="tok-bind">@vec</span>)
  <span class="tok-kw">FILTER</span> sim &gt; <span class="tok-num">0.8</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">SORT</span> sim <span class="tok-kw">DESC</span> <span class="tok-kw">LIMIT</span> <span class="tok-num">10</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> { title: doc.title, score: sim }</pre>
      </div>
      <div class="shape">
        <div class="shape-head"><b>Hybrid</b><span>vector index + fulltext field, one call</span></div>
        <pre class="well"><span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> r <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> <span class="tok-fn">HYBRID_SEARCH</span>(<span class="tok-str">"articles"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"embedding_idx"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"content"</span>, <span class="tok-bind">@vec</span>, <span class="tok-str">"machine learning"</span>,
    { vector_weight: <span class="tok-num">0.8</span>, text_weight: <span class="tok-num">0.2</span>, limit: <span class="tok-num">5</span> })
  <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> r</pre>
      </div>
      <div class="shape">
        <div class="shape-head"><b>Time-series</b><span>requests per hour</span></div>
        <pre class="well"><span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> e <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> events
  <span class="tok-kw">COLLECT</span> bucket = <span class="tok-fn">TIME_BUCKET</span>(e.ts, <span class="tok-str">'1h'</span>)
  <span class="tok-kw">AGGREGATE</span> hits = <span class="tok-fn">COUNT</span>(<span class="tok-num">1</span>)
  <span class="tok-kw">SORT</span> bucket <span class="tok-kw">ASC</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> { bucket, hits }</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ LIMITS ============================ -->
<section class="sec sec-band" id="limits">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>What it will not do for you.</h2>
      <p>Written down here so you find them before production does. Each one is also in the docs.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="limits">
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>A checkpoint is not an off-site backup.</h3>
        <p><code>POST /_api/backup</code> hard-links SST files on the same volume. It protects against a bad write or a dropped collection, not against losing the disk. On a cluster it covers the local node only. Copy it elsewhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>Replication is eventually consistent.</h3>
        <p>Nodes replicate master to master, and writes queue for a node that is offline. Until the queue drains, two nodes can disagree about the same document.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>Every query has a ceiling.</h3>
        <p>An HTTP query runs under a 30-second deadline and may materialise at most 5,000,000 intermediate rows (<code>SOLIDB_MAX_INTERMEDIATE_ROWS</code>). A query that needs more fails; the server keeps running.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>No job queue for your application.</h3>
        <p>SoliDB schedules its own work: trigger dispatch, embedding generation, view refresh. Your background jobs and cron belong in your application framework.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>One unique-index edge case remains.</h3>
        <p>Deleting the document that holds a unique value and inserting another with the same value, in one transaction, is refused. Do those in two transactions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <h3>The 2.0 storage migration is one-way.</h3>
        <p>A 1.x binary cannot open a data directory once its collections have moved. Take a checkpoint first, and have free disk of 1.2 × your largest collection plus 1 GiB.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- =========================== RELEASES =========================== -->
<section class="sec" id="releases">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>Recent releases, and what each one fixed.</h2>
      <p>Each one starts from the bug, not the feature: in a post where there is one, otherwise in its changelog entry. The full list is in the <a class="text-link" href="/docs/changelog">changelog</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="releases">
      <a class="release" href="/blog/solidb-2-7-the-queries-that-scanned-anyway">
        <span class="release-ver">2.6 – 2.7</span>
        <span class="release-title">The queries that scanned with an index right there</span>
        <span class="release-sum">A job claim read 122,050 rows to find 50; now 0.21&nbsp;ms. Compound prefixes, <code>!=</code> and <code>OR</code> read from indexes, a sparse index that dropped null rows from <code>&lt;=</code> and <code>SORT</code>, non-sparse and trigram indexes.</span>
      </a>
      <a class="release" href="/blog/solidb-2-3-1-the-index-in-the-second-filter">
        <span class="release-ver">2.3.1</span>
        <span class="release-title">The index in the second FILTER</span>
        <span class="release-sum">The planner only searched the FILTER right after the <code>FOR</code> for an index. A query whose indexed field sat in the next one read all 200,000 events; on 2.3.1 it reads 27,294.</span>
      </a>
      <a class="release" href="/blog/solidb-2-3-answers-that-outlived-their-data">
        <span class="release-ver">2.3.0</span>
        <span class="release-title">Answers that outlived their data</span>
        <span class="release-sum">A dropped and recreated database still answered with its old rows from the query cache, and <code>UPDATE</code> could not move an edge. Query latency is on <code>/metrics</code>; a two-phase commit that never wrote is gone.</span>
      </a>
      <a class="release" href="/docs/changelog#v2-2-1">
        <span class="release-ver">2.2.1</span>
        <span class="release-title">A daemonized server never gave memory back</span>
        <span class="release-sum"><code>--daemon</code> forks, and the fork stopped jemalloc's background purger. A dev server up five days held 9.2 GB against 918 MB allocated; on 2.2.1 it sits at 854 MB.</span>
      </a>
      <a class="release" href="/docs/changelog#v2-2-0">
        <span class="release-ver">2.2.0</span>
        <span class="release-title">Audio and video blobs that seek</span>
        <span class="release-sum">Blob downloads answer <code>Range</code> requests with 206, and iOS Safari would not play media without it. RocksDB moves to 11.8.1, and a data directory still opens with the previous binary.</span>
      </a>
      <a class="release" href="/blog/solidb-2-1-transactions-conflicts-roles">
        <span class="release-ver">2.1.0</span>
        <span class="release-title">Things it said it did, and did not quite do</span>
        <span class="release-sum">A driver transaction that committed on the spot, two writes of one unique value that both went through, a custom role that granted nothing.</span>
      </a>
      <a class="release" href="/blog/solidb-2-0-shared-keyspace">
        <span class="release-ver">2.0.0</span>
        <span class="release-title">One column family for every collection</span>
        <span class="release-sum">Creating a collection cost 0.2 s on a large instance, because each one rewrote a 6.3 MB OPTIONS file. It now costs a few milliseconds at any size.</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========================= QUICK START ========================= -->
<section class="sec" id="start">
  <div class="wrap qs">
    <div>
      <h2>Build it and run it.</h2>
      <p>
        The storage engine is pure Rust, but a few dependencies are C (zstd, jemalloc),
        so the first build needs a C toolchain. The server listens on port 6745 and prints a random admin
        password to its log on first boot.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Client libraries: Rust, Go, Python, JavaScript (Node and browser), PHP, Ruby,
        Elixir, Laravel Eloquent, and a mobile SDK. Or plain HTTP and <code>curl</code>.
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/clients">Clients in the docs</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <pre class="shell"><span class="dim"># Debian / Ubuntu build dependencies</span>
$ apt-get install build-essential clang libclang-dev \
    pkg-config libssl-dev libzstd-dev

$ git clone https://github.com/solisoft/solidb
$ cd solidb &amp;&amp; cargo install --path .
$ solidb --port 6745 --data-dir ./data</pre>
  </div>
</section>

</main>

